Here is a shameless plug for my project. It is a esp32 based programmable keyboard (HID) with a web interface and api. It can be directly flashed to the m5stack atom or m5stack cardputer.<p>One stand-out feature it has is an implementation of the Keepass KDBX database standard. The database can be stored on FRAM or SD card and can be unlocked via the web interface. The key is stored in volatile memory.
There is a DSL scripting language that can be used to run macros, programs, or functions. With the database unlocked you can send credentials via key-value DSL strings such as the following example:
{credstore  myusername}
{TAB}
{credstore mypassword}
{ENTER}<p><a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;akoerner&#x2F;kprox" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;akoerner&#x2F;kprox</a>
